    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, parallel addition that mirrors the existing telephone-uniqueness feature almost exactly:\n\n- `CheckEmailUnique` sits in `function/owner/` alongside `CheckTelephoneUnique`, follows the same `Check<X>` naming, same `service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto, OwnerRepository)` signature, same loop-and-throw-checked-exception shape. The email-optional guard is a justified, well-commented deviation appropriate to the spec.\n- `DuplicateEmailException` / `DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler` are faithful counterparts to the telephone pair \u2014 checked exception carrying the offending value, handler building an RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail` through the shared `ProblemDetails` helper with an `errors` property. Even the (slightly unusual) fully-qualified `java.util.List.of` matches the telephone handler's style, so it stays consistent rather than introducing a new idiom.\n- The escalation YAML and the `owners.POST.yml` wiring insert the new step in the correct place (after `checkTelephoneUnique`, before `checkHouseholdUnique`), with `govern: [ transaction ]` like its siblings.\n\nLogic is in the right unit, nothing is duplicated beyond the deliberate pattern-matching, and no class is becoming a catch-all. A maintainer would find this entirely natural.\n\nOne tiny, non-blocking observation (not a structural defect): `ValidateNewOwner` already lower-cases the request email with `toLowerCase()`, while `CheckEmailUnique` re-normalizes with `toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)`. The re-normalization is harmless/defensive, but the two call sites use different locale forms. Not worth a change on its own.",
